  If you're playing on your own machine, maybe.  

  Be VERY CAREFUL if you try this.  I'm not sure that I recommend it.
It's fraught with peril of losing your game without even playing it.

  I just discovered how to really make use of Explore Mode.  

  Start the game with "nethack -X"

   - This will start up NetHack using your save file, but in explore 
     mode.
   - The first question you will be asked is:  "Do you want to keep the 
     save file?"  Say yes.
   - Your save file is still intact.
     - It's not set to Explore Mode.
     - You can now test out your entire situation without the tedium
       of setting it up in wizmode.
     - I have to believe Explore Mode works this way for a reason.

  You can then play through the rest of the game trying things out. 
Explore mode asks permission before it will let you die.  (Some people
have done this in wizmode.  It's a lot of trouble to set up the same
starting conditions.)

  There are a number of critical points:

  - If you save the game, BOOM, your old game is gone.  
    - You're stuck in explore mode.
  - So #quit out.
    - Be SURE you're in Explore mode first.  
    - Press 'X'.  If you're in Explore Mode nothing will happen.
      
  Nothing you do will be saved.  OTOH you've nothing to lose.  Just
scamper on through.  

  Yes, this can be abused to identify your current inventory.  I don't
think that's a problem for you right now.

  It can also be used to explore a level in advance, safely.

  Those are not the great unbalancing effects they might appear.    
   - Nothing you do in explore mode will last, 
     - so this is much less effective than save scumming.  
   - If you die while not in explore mode, you're dead.

  The real balancer for using Explore Mode this way:  The first time you
die in real mode when you thought you were in Explore Mode.

  So this is a great way to learn what does what, and how.  Especially
to just try out what you've got to see what it does.  Finally, it's
possible to learn NetHack without spoiling yourself silly or dying every
time you get to a part of the game that's new to you.


  This method is also great for trying out different escape methods in
difficult situations.  

  
  Again, the risk here is destroying your game inadvertently.  The
reward is just blowing through a perilous situation learning as much as
possible without risk.  This offsets the usual problem in NetHack: "Die
and Learn" is frustrating and inefficient on the Ascension Run.

  Alternatively, you can make a copy of your nethack directory.  Delete
the save file there.  Start there in wizmode: "Nethack -u wizard -D".
Use the #levelchange command to get to XL:30.  Use <CTRL-W> to wish up
everything.  Wish for the amulet of Yendor.  Escape the dungeon and try
the Elemental and Astral Planes.  It will make a big difference.



  Finally, the big things you want to have when you leave the dungeon:
   - the real Amulet of Yendor.
     - Players have left without it. "Escaped the Dungeon."
   - 4+ scrolls of gold detection.
   - a method of confusing yourself.


  


